  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how autophagy- and lysosome-related compartments are distributed across major brain cell types and neuronal compartments. A central neuron is shown with its soma, dendrites, and axon surrounded by astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, and microglia, with a nearby blood vessel indicating the brain tissue environment.\n\nColored dots represent different stages of the degradative pathway, including phagophores, autophagosomes, amphisomes, autolysosomes, lysosomes, and liposome-like structures. These organelles appear throughout neurons and glia, but the figure emphasizes that each cell type handles somewhat different cargo. In the neuronal soma, labels highlight protein aggregates, mitochondria, and ER; dendrites are associated with postsynaptic proteins, receptors, and spine remodeling; axons contain synaptic proteins and vesicles. Astrocytes also contain mitochondria, ER, and protein aggregates, while oligodendrocytes are linked to mitochondria and protein aggregates.\n\nMicroglia are depicted somewhat differently, containing α-synuclein and amyloid-β within degradative compartments, suggesting their role in clearing extracellular or disease-related material. Overall, the image conveys a brain-wide network of compartment-specific autophagy and lysosomal processing that differs across neural cell types and neuronal subregions.",
